India has some of the richest dinosaur nesting grounds in the world. Around Dhar in [[@madhya-pradesh|Madhya Pradesh]], scientists recorded 92 nests holding 256 titanosaur eggs, described in 2023. The nests were close together, like a nesting colony of seabirds.

The eggs were laid in shallow pits and buried, much as [[@sea-turtles|sea turtles]] bury theirs on beaches today. Shell structure suggests the nests were covered, warmed by the soil or rotting plants. One egg from Dhar, reported in 2022, had a second eggshell inside it, an "egg within an egg". This happens in birds too, and it was the first time it had been found in a dinosaur.

{{story:king-cobra}}

The Andaman Islands, in the Bay of Bengal, sit near the boundary where the Indian plate dives under the Burma plate. On Baratang Island, visitors walk through forest to reach a clearing where small cones of grey mud bubble quietly.

After the giant [[@earthquakes|earthquake]] and [[tsunami|tsunami]] of December 2004, scientists noticed changes. In 2005, one of the Baratang mud volcanoes erupted strongly, building a new mound of mud and covering trees nearby. The [[@andaman|Andaman Islands]] are the only place in India with known active mud volcanoes.

{{story:earthquakes}}

In April 1961, the Soviet Union sent [[@first-in-space|Yuri Gagarin]] into space, the first person ever to go there. The United States was badly shaken. A few weeks later, President John F. Kennedy told the US Congress that America should land a man on the Moon and return him safely before the decade was out.

It was a huge gamble. At the time, the US had sent just one astronaut into space, on a 15-minute hop. The Moon landing became the biggest prize of the [[@space-race|space race]] between the two countries.

{{story:space-race}}

The smallest bird in the world is the [[@extreme-smallest-animals|bee hummingbird]] of Cuba. It is about 5 to 6 cm long and weighs about 2 g, less than a one-rupee coin. Its eggs are about the size of a pea.

Hovering burns huge amounts of energy. A hummingbird's heart can beat more than 1,000 times a minute while it feeds. To keep going, it visits hundreds of flowers a day, sipping sweet [[nectar|nectar]] with a long tongue that can flick in and out many times a second. It also snaps up tiny insects and spiders for [[protein|protein]].

{{story:sugar}}

In 2011, a team of engineers from the Netherlands and the USA built an odd-looking bike. Its wheels had extra discs spinning the opposite way to cancel the top effect, and its fork was set up so the wheel didn't trail behind in the usual way. Pushed along with no rider, it still balanced itself.

The secret was where its weight sat. A heavy weight placed high and forward on the front made the front end swing into a lean quickly. The lesson was that many details of a bike's design work together, and no single one explains everything.

{{story:bicycles-history}}

The oldest flutes yet found come from caves in southwestern Germany. One was carved from the wing bone of a griffon vulture, with five finger holes, and is thought to be about 35,000 to 40,000 years old. People were making music on flutes long before they were farming or writing.

Since then, almost every culture has made some kind of flute. Japan has the bamboo shakuhachi. Native American peoples made flutes of cedar and river cane. Children in many US schools learn the recorder, which has a little channel that shapes the breath for you, much like a [[@whistles|whistle]].

{{story:bamboo}}

Festivals tied to the Moon move around on a wall calendar. [[@diwali-lights|Diwali]] falls on a new Moon night, so it can be anywhere from mid-October to mid-November. Holi comes on a full Moon in February or March.

Festivals tied to the Sun hardly move at all. Makar Sankranti marks the Sun entering the sign of Makara (Capricorn), and it falls on about 14 January almost every year. In Gujarat that day is Uttarayan, when the [[@kites-festival|sky fills with kites]]. Pongal in Tamil Nadu and Vishu in Kerala are also set by the Sun.

{{story:diwali-lights}}

The name Ghibli comes from an Italian word for a hot wind that blows off the Sahara. It was also the name of an Italian aeroplane, and Miyazaki has loved aircraft since childhood, when his father's family business made aircraft parts. The founders hoped to blow a new [[@wind-blows|wind]] through Japanese animation.

{{story:wind-blows}}

The studio's logo is Totoro, a huge, furry forest spirit from My Neighbour Totoro (1988). In the film, two sisters move to the countryside and discover him in the woods. In its best-known scene, they wait beside him at a rainy bus stop.

At Oxford University in the late 1930s, Howard Florey and Ernst Chain picked up Fleming's forgotten paper. With the chemist Norman Heatley, they found ways to grow the mould and squeeze out tiny amounts of pure penicillin. Their lab used bathtubs, milk churns and bedpans as growing trays.

In 1941 they treated their first patient, a policeman named Albert Alexander with a badly infected face. He began to recover within days. Then the penicillin ran out, even though they were collecting it from his urine to reuse, and he died. The drug clearly worked. The problem was making enough.

{{story:microscopes}}

When Europeans arrived in the Americas after 1492, they brought diseases such as smallpox and measles. The Indigenous peoples had never met them and had no resistance. [[epidemic|Epidemics]] killed a very large share of the population over the following century, though exact numbers are hotly debated.

Disease helped [[@spanish-conquest|small groups of Spanish soldiers]] conquer the Aztec and Inca empires. It emptied villages ahead of settlers. Few events in history have been shaped so heavily by germs.

{{story:black-death}}

The fingerprint is called a hash. A hash takes any piece of data, such as a list of payments, and turns it into a short string of letters and numbers. Change even one comma and the hash comes out completely different.

Each block stores the hash of the block before it. If someone alters an old block, its hash changes, and it no longer matches the next block. The chain visibly breaks. This clever use of [[@cryptography-maths|cryptography]], the maths of [[@cipher-codes|secret codes]], is what makes blockchains hard to tamper with.

{{story:cryptography-maths}}

On those early machines, the typebars sat in a circle beneath the paper. If you hit two neighbouring keys quickly, their arms could tangle and stick. The popular explanation says Sholes moved common letter pairs, like T and H, apart to reduce jams.

It's a neat story, but the evidence is mixed. In 2011, two researchers at [[@kyoto|Kyoto]] University, Koichi and Motoko Yasuoka, studied old [[@patents|patents]] and records. They argued the layout was shaped largely by telegraph operators, who used early typewriters to type out [[@codes-morse|Morse code]] messages as they heard them. Some letters that are easy to confuse in Morse were placed close together. Other historians still favour the jamming idea, and both may be part of the story.

{{story:alphabet-history}}

Wikipedia has three main content rules. Articles must have a neutral point of view, which means describing all the important views fairly without taking sides. Every claim should be verifiable, so readers can check it against a reliable published source. And there is no original research: you can't add your own new discovery, only what trusted sources have already reported.

That last rule surprises people. A scientist who has made a new finding still has to wait until it is published elsewhere before it belongs on Wikipedia. The encyclopedia is meant to collect knowledge, not create it. When editors disagree, they argue it out on a "talk page" behind each article, and the debate stays there for anyone to read.

{{story:encyclopedias}}

At a site called Ohalo II, on the shore of the Sea of Galilee in Israel, archaeologists found the remains of brush huts about 23,000 years old. The floors held seeds, fish bones and stone tools. The site survived because it was flooded and buried in mud.

In Ukraine, at Mezhyrich, people built round huts from [[@mammoths|mammoth]] bones about 15,000 years ago. Big jawbones and leg bones formed the walls, probably covered with hides. A single hut could use bones from many [[mammoth|mammoths]], some probably collected from animals that had died naturally. In a land with few trees, bones were the best building material around.

{{story:mammoths}}

The assembly met about 40 times a year on the Pnyx, a rocky hill facing the [[@acropolis|Acropolis]]. Any citizen could come, and some votes needed at least 6,000 to be valid. Anyone could stand up and speak. Votes were usually by a show of hands, and officials judged which side won by eye.

Athens may have had around 30,000 adult male citizens in the 400s BCE. That is fewer than the number of people who can sit in a large cricket stadium today. In India's [[@election-commission|general election]] in 2024, by comparison, more than 60 crore people cast a vote.

{{story:vote-how}}

The best-known leader was Toussaint Louverture. He had been born into slavery, learned to read, and gained his freedom before the revolution. He turned out to be a brilliant general and a sharp politician. He switched sides between Spain and France when it helped his cause. His chosen surname, Louverture, means "the opening" in French. One popular story says it came from his knack for finding gaps in enemy lines.

France abolished slavery in its colonies in 1794, and Toussaint fought for France after that. By 1801 he governed the whole colony and wrote it a [[constitution|constitution]] that banned slavery for ever.

{{story:mosquito-diseases}}

Rescuers waded in as quickly as they could, but the molasses fought back. As the syrup spread out and cooled in the winter air, it became thicker and thicker.

In 2016, scientists at Harvard University tested this with corn syrup in a cold room. They found that the spreading molasses would have cooled fast at its edges, turning almost like glue. That helps explain why people trapped in it couldn't escape. Some liquids behave in even stranger ways, like the ones described in [[@non-newtonian|non-Newtonian fluids]].

{{story:non-newtonian}}

On 4 October 1957, the Soviet Union launched Sputnik 1. It was a shiny metal ball about 58 cm across, with four long antennas. It did little more than send out a beeping radio signal as it circled Earth every 96 minutes. Radio fans around the world could hear it.

Americans were alarmed. If the Soviets could put a ball in orbit, could their rockets carry bombs across the world? In 1958 the USA created [[@nasa-centres|NASA]] to run its space programme and poured money into science education.

{{story:sputnik}}

Some places are recognised by many countries but not all. Kosovo declared independence from Serbia in 2008. Over 100 countries recognise it, but others, including India, do not.

Taiwan has its own government, army and elections. [[@china-today|China]] says it is part of China, and most countries, including India and the USA, do not officially recognise it as a separate country. Places like these are why the count is never settled. Each case is tied up with history and politics, and governments disagree strongly.

{{story:borders-why}}

Premchand first wrote in [[@urdu|Urdu]] under the name Nawab Rai. Soon after his first collection of stories, Soz-e-Watan, came out, it was seized by the British authorities. The stories spoke of love for the homeland, and officials thought they could stir up rebellion against British rule. Copies were burned.

After that, he began publishing as Premchand. He also moved more and more into [[@hindi-language|Hindi]], though he kept writing in both languages. He used a simple, spoken style that ordinary readers in either language could follow.

{{story:villages-india}}

For centuries in India, oil came from a ghani, also called a kolhu: a big wooden or stone mortar with a heavy pestle turned by a walking bullock. Seeds were slowly crushed, and oil trickled out. Mustard oil made this way is sold as *kachi ghani*. It is stronger in smell and taste.

Big factories today crush seeds with steel screw presses. To get every last drop, many also use a solvent, a liquid that [[dissolve|dissolves]] the oil out of the crushed seeds and is then removed. The oil is then usually refined to make it clear, pale and long-lasting.

{{story:rainforests}}
